course: dictionary
lesson: 1112
title: Braw and other Scots words
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words belong to one place. Braw is a Scots word, from Scotland, and it means fine, good, lovely or handsome. You can use context to guess it. Morven, 16, is given some glittery hair clips and says, «Ah, they're braw, cheers, wee yin.» She is pleased and thankful, so braw must be a nice word.
explain: Check your guess by swapping in a word that means the same. Scots people may say «It's a braw day» for sunny weather. Try lovely in place of braw: «It's a lovely day for sunny weather» still makes sense, so your guess is right.
explain: Braw is an adjective, so it describes a noun, like a day or a pair of clips. Its forms are braw, brawer and brawest. Scots is one of many ways English is spoken. When Sophie-Grace in Toronto hears her gran say, «Ye're lookin' braw, hen!», she answers, «Huh?» Not understanding is normal. Ask, guess from context, then check in a dictionary.
fact: «Braw» means fine, good, lovely or handsome.
fact: «Braw» is a Scots word, heard in Scotland and in Scottish stories and songs.
fact: «Braw» is an adjective, so it describes a noun, as in «a braw day».
fact: Scottish people may say «That's braw» to mean great.
fact: Scots is a dialect, a way of speaking English that differs from place to place.
exercise: Morven says, «Ah, they're braw, cheers, wee yin.» What does braw mean here? | Broken | *Lovely | Very far away | Cold and wet
exercise: Sophie-Grace's gran says, «Ye're lookin' braw, hen!» What is she telling her? | *She thinks she looks lovely | She says she is late | She is asking for help | She wants her to sit down
exercise: Which Scots word means fine, good or lovely? | howay | *braw | butt | skint
test: Morven's friend says, «Ah, they're braw.» What is she saying about the hair clips? | They are lost | They are broken | They are very old | *They are lovely
test: What part of speech is braw? | verb | noun | adverb | *adjective
exam: Which is the best check for a sentence with braw in it? | Skip the sentence and never come back | Count the letters in the word | *Put lovely in its place and see if the sentence still makes sense | Put sad in its place
